[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Bronstein-Larsen Variation"]

{Caro-Kann Defense: Bronstein-Larsen Variation. A slight inaccuracy in move order — the bishop is better placed before this pawn commits.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 {Book so far. The plan is the usual one: finish development, then decide which side to open.} 6. c3 Bf5 7. Bd3 Bg6 8. Ne2 ! e6 {The point of the whole plan. Material is level, but only one side has a target to attack.} (8... c5 Rb1 c4 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. O-O Bd6 10. Bf4 Qc7 11. Qd2 Nd7 ?! 12. Rad1 Rg8 13. Bxd6 Qxd6 14. Qf4 Qxf4 15. Nxf4 O-O-O 16. Rfe1 Nb6 17. Nxg6 hxg6 18. h4 f5 19. g3 Nd5 20. Kg2 Kc7 21. Bc4 Nf6 22. Re2 b5 23. Bb3 a5 24. a4 bxa4 25. Bxa4 Rb8 26. b3 Rgd8 27. Re5 Nd5 28. c4 Nc3 29. Ra1 Rxd4 30. Rxa5 Rd2 31. Ra7+ Rb7 32. Ra6 Rb6 33. Ra7+ Rb7 34. Ra6 Nxa4 35. R1xa4 Rxb3 36. Ra8 Rbb2 37. R4a7+ Rb7 38. Rxb7+ Kxb7 39. Rf8 Rd7 40. c5 Ka6 41. Rb8 Rb7 42. Rg8 Kb5 43. f3 Kxc5 44. g4 fxg4 45. fxg4 Kd4 46. h5 gxh5 47. gxh5 Rb5 48. Rg4+ Ke5 49. h6 {The rest is technique, which is another way of saying it still has to be played accurately.} Kf6 0-1

